WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
This is the brief on Gandhi's 1930 Salt March.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Back in 1930, a simple 240-mile walk to the ocean to make salt literally shook the mighty

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
British Empire, giving the world a permanent blueprint for how peaceful protest defeats oppression.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.000
First, we gotta look at the strategy.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.000
The British had this strict monopoly on salt, right?

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.000
They artificially jacked up the prices and taxed it heavily.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.000
And salt isn't just a spice, we literally need it to survive.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.000
Gandhi chose salt because this injustice affected absolutely everyone.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.000
He used it to introduce satyagraha, or truth force.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.000
Think of it kind of like a school bully demanding you pay them just to drink from the water fountain.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Gandhi realized if everyone peacefully refused to pay, well, that bully would totally lose their power.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.000
Second, let's look at the action itself.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:59.000
In March 1930, Gandhi and 78 followers walked 240 miles to the coastal town of Dandi.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Once there, he just picked up a lump of salty mud, illegally making his own salt.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.000
This simple act sparked a massive nationwide uprising.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Over 60,000 Indians were thrown in jail.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.000
So did they fight back?

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.000
No way.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:19.000
They took the punishment completely peacefully, which absolutely baffled the British authorities.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Finally, there's the massive global impact.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.000
The march didn't win Indian independence overnight, but it completely destroyed the empire's reputation.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.000
You see, journalists captured the brutal beatings of peaceful protesters, exposing this cruelty to the whole world.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:40.000
This exact blueprint of truth and self-suffering went on to directly inspire global civil rights

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.000
legends like Martin Luther King Jr. and Nelson Mandela.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Ultimately, the Salt March proved beyond a shadow of a doubt that true, world-changing

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.000
power comes from peaceful unity, not violence.
